# Linker fragments for the FOC hot path.
#
# Wired in via LDFRAGMENTS in main/CMakeLists.txt. `noflash` places an archive's
# text and rodata into internal RAM so it is not fetched through the flash cache.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS SMALL, and what it can and cannot do:
#
# The sampling ISR and the FOC task both live in sensorless.o, because
# foc_sampler.hpp is a header and espp's BldcDriver::set_pwm() is a header
# inline — the bldc_driver archive itself is only 22 bytes of text, so placing it
# here does almost nothing on its own. Linker fragments map whole
# archives/objects, not individual functions, and sensorless.o is ~142 KB of
# text, most of it cold console and command-parsing code. Placing it wholesale
# would consume more than half the remaining internal RAM to speed up code that
# runs once.
#
# So the hot functions are marked IRAM_ATTR directly (see foc_sampler.hpp and
# foc_task_fn in sensorless.cpp), which is function-granular and drags the
# inlined set_pwm along with them. This file covers the supporting archives that
# the ISR path calls into and that ARE separate translation units.
#
# If the hot path is ever split into its own .cpp, add it here as:
#     archive: libmain.a
#     entries:
#         foc_hot (noflash)
#
# MEASURED CONTEXT (2026-08-04): `late%` is NOT limited by cache or fetch speed.
# Raising the instruction cache 16 KB -> 32 KB cut cmax from ~220 us to ~65 us and
# isr_max from ~22 us to ~13 us, while late% did not move at all. late% is
# dominated by ISR ENTRY latency (the `gap` test in foc_sampler.hpp), i.e. the
# interrupt being held off by other drivers' critical sections. IRAM reduces how
# long the ISR takes once it runs; it does not make it start sooner. The lever
# for entry latency is MCPWM intr_priority, which espp's BldcDriver does not
# currently expose.

[mapping:foc_adc]
archive: libespp__adc.a
entries:
    * (noflash)

[mapping:foc_bldc_driver]
archive: libespp__bldc_driver.a
entries:
    * (noflash)
